course: adult-life
lesson: 13
title: Dates, engagements and weddings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Grown-ups who like each other a lot "go out" together, which means they are boyfriend and girlfriend, or partners, and get to know each other to see whether they want to stay together for a long time.
explain: Some couples get engaged, which is a promise to marry, then plan a wedding, and after it go away on a honeymoon; some couples never marry but stay together, and all of these are normal.
explain: Picture a wedding in a hotel near Galway: your cousin is a bridesmaid in a matching dress, Uncle Dev shakes over his best man's speech, a guest whispers "about time, they've been together for years", and Mum dabs her eyes saying "happy tears".
explain: Even married parents need time just for the two of them, so on date night a trusted babysitter comes, and that does not mean they want to get away from you.
fact: "Going out with someone" means two people are a couple who spend time together because they like each other a lot.
fact: Date night is when a couple goes out together, just the two of them, which keeps their friendship strong.
fact: A babysitter is a trusted person who looks after children while parents are out, and parents leave phone numbers and rules so the babysitter knows who to call.
fact: Older relatives often ask young people "Have you got a boyfriend or girlfriend yet?" because they are interested, but many people find it embarrassing, and "Not right now" is a fine answer.
fact: Many grown-ups meet partners through dating apps, which are for adults only; children should never meet anyone they only know from the internet.
fact: Being engaged means two people have promised to marry each other, and a ring is often given as a sign of the promise.
fact: An engagement can last months or years while the couple saves money and plans the wedding.
fact: "It's about time" at a wedding means the couple have been together a long time and people wondered when they would marry.
fact: The best man is usually the groom's closest friend or brother; he helps on the day and makes a speech, which makes many people nervous.
fact: Bridesmaids are friends or relatives who help the bride and walk with her, and children can be flower girls or page boys.
fact: Grown-ups sometimes cry "happy tears" when they feel strong happy feelings like love or pride.
fact: A honeymoon is a holiday a couple takes just after the wedding, to rest and celebrate, just the two of them.
exercise: Aunt Jo says "I'm going out with someone new." What does she mean? | She has a new boyfriend, girlfriend or partner | She is going to the shops with a new friend from work | She has started a new job | She is moving out to a new house
exercise: Mum and Dad say "It's date night." What is happening? | They are going out together, just the two of them | They are choosing a date on the calendar for a family party | They are going to eat dates from the shop | They are going to a parents' evening at school
exercise: Why do parents have date nights? | To have time to talk and have fun together | Because they want to get away from the children | Because the babysitter needs the money | Because the law says they must go out weekly
exercise: Why does Mum leave a list of phone numbers by the kettle for the babysitter? | So the babysitter knows who to call if anything happens | So the babysitter can order pizza for everyone from the list of shops | So the babysitter can phone her friends | So you can call your friends all evening
exercise: Gran asks your big cousin "Have you got a boyfriend yet?" and he goes red. Why? | Relationships feel private, so the question is embarrassing | He is ill and has a fever | He is angry with Gran for being rude to him on purpose at the table | The room is too hot because of the oven
exercise: What is a good answer if someone asks you about something private? | A polite "Not right now" | A made-up story to make them stop | Shouting at them to go away | Running out of the room
exercise: Uncle Sam says "We met on an app." What does he mean? | He met his partner through a dating app on his phone | He met his partner while fixing a broken phone in his shop | He met his partner inside a video game | He met his partner on an aeroplane
exercise: Who are dating apps for? | Adults only | Children over eight | Anyone who has a phone | Only people who are married
exercise: Your aunt shows a ring and says "We're engaged!" What does it mean? | They have promised to marry each other | They have just got married in secret at the town hall | They are busy and cannot talk | They have bought a ring to sell
exercise: Why can an engagement last a long time? | The couple may be saving money and planning the wedding | Engagements must last exactly five years | The ring takes years to make by hand | They are waiting for all the children in the family to grow up first
exercise: A guest whispers "They've been together for years, it's about time." What does it mean? | People wondered when this long-time couple would marry | The wedding has started late because the bride got lost | The guest wants to know the time | The couple met only last week
exercise: What does a best man do? | Helps the groom on the day and makes a speech | Marries the bride instead of the groom | Cooks the wedding meal | Drives the couple on their honeymoon
exercise: Why is Uncle Dev worried about being best man? | Speaking in front of many people makes him nervous | He thinks he will have to marry someone | He has to pay for the whole wedding | He does not like the couple
exercise: What is a bridesmaid? | A friend or relative who helps the bride and walks with her | A woman who is paid to clean the church before and after the wedding | The bride's older sister, always | The person who bakes the wedding cake
exercise: Mum cries at the wedding and says "happy tears". What does she mean? | She is crying because she feels strong happy feelings | She is secretly sad about the wedding and doesn't want it to happen | She has hurt herself and is pretending | She has something in her eye from the confetti
exercise: "They're off on honeymoon to Greece." What is a honeymoon? | A holiday a couple takes just after the wedding | A trip to visit bee farms | A holiday for the whole wedding party and all the guests | A month of eating honey after a wedding
test: Your little brother asks "is date night cos they're sick of us?" Put him right. | No, couples need time together to stay close | Yes, parents go out to get away from children | Yes, but only when children are naughty | No, date night is when they pay the bills
test: "Numbers by the kettle, bedtime half eight, no screens after seven, back by eleven." Who is Mum most likely talking to? | The babysitter | The plumber | Your teacher | The neighbours' dog
test: Gran at Sunday lunch: "So, any girlfriend then?" Your cousin mumbles "Gran!" and stares at his plate. What is the kind thing to do? | Change the subject and let him keep it private | Keep asking until he answers | Laugh and tell everyone at school | Ask Gran to tell everyone at the table all of his secrets
test: A girl in your class says "my sister's meeting a boy off the internet, so I can too". Is that right? | No, children should never meet anyone they only know online | Yes, if the boy seems nice in his messages | Yes, as long as it is in the daytime | No, but only because the internet is slow
test: overheard on the phone: "Did you see the rock on her finger?? Finally! When's the big day?" What is the gossip about? | Someone has got engaged with a ring | Someone has found a big stone in the garden | Someone has hurt her finger | Someone is going rock climbing
test: Your cousin says "engaged means they're married already". What is true? | No, engaged means they have promised to marry later | Yes, engaged and married mean exactly the same thing | Yes, but only if there is a ring | No, engaged means they have split up
test: At the reception Auntie Pat leans over: "Eleven years! I thought he'd never ask." Is she being mean? | No, it is usually friendly wedding gossip about a long wait | Yes, she is trying to stop the wedding | Yes, she wants the couple to split up and is saying so in front of everyone | No, she is reading out the menu
test: Uncle Dev keeps saying his jokes to the mirror and muttering "they'll hate it". How can you help? | Listen to his practice speech and laugh at the jokes | Tell him the speech will be terrible | Hide his notes so he can't read them | Tell him best men never speak
test: Your brother says "Mum's crying, the wedding must be going wrong". Put him right. | Grown-ups can cry happy tears when they feel love and pride | Crying always means something has gone badly wrong with the day | She is crying because the food was cold | She is crying because weddings are boring
test: "Two weeks in Santorini, just the two of 'em, lucky so-and-sos." What are they going on? | Their honeymoon | A school trip | A business trip for work | A family holiday with all the cousins
test: At a wedding, which person is a page boy most like? | A bridesmaid, but a young boy | The best man's father | The waiter at the reception | The person who marries the couple
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the state of being engaged to be married. | Two persons have promised to marry one another | Two persons have been lawfully married by a registrar | Two persons have resolved to separate | Two persons have been introduced for the first time
exam: Identify the principal function of the best man upon the occasion of a wedding. | To assist the groom and deliver a speech | To officiate at the ceremony of marriage | To prepare the wedding breakfast | To conduct the couple upon their honeymoon
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "honeymoon"? | A holiday undertaken by a couple immediately subsequent to their wedding | A period of one month during which honey is consumed | A celebration held prior to an engagement | A holiday undertaken by all guests of the wedding
exam: Ascertain the course of conduct appropriate to a minor in respect of persons known solely through the internet. | Such persons ought never to be met in person | Such persons may be met provided it is daylight | Such persons may be met provided their messages are courteous | Such persons ought to be met only in the company of a friend
